# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/

# Tooling
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Env / OS
.env
.DS_Store

# Scratch run output. NOTE: this ignores result documents produced while developing
# poison-garden itself. SUBMITTED results live in results/ and ARE committed — that is
# the leaderboard, and an ignored one records nothing.
/tmp-results/

# AI workflow artifacts (local only, not part of the published repo)
CLAUDE.md
SPEC.md
tasks/
changes/
# Working design notes stay local; anything the README or CONTRIBUTING links to must ship.
# A blanket `docs/` silently swallowed docs/tier-reliability.md, which three shipped files
# link to — the same defect as the unanchored `*.lock` below, which swallowed two specimens'
# data files. `test_linked_documents_are_tracked` now fails the build instead.
docs/design/

# Scan artifacts written into the repo root by third-party scanners run against the
# corpus during development (e.g. `frisk scan` drops a frisk.lock in cwd). These are
# not ours and must never be committed — a frisk baseline living inside the corpus that
# measures frisk is exactly the wrong artifact to ship.
frisk.lock
# Anchored to the repo ROOT. An unanchored `*.lock` matches at every depth, which silently
# swallowed `specimens/*/toolchain.lock` and `deps.lock` — shipped DATA for two hygiene
# specimens, not lockfiles. Both crashed on import for everyone who cloned, while passing
# locally, and the published corpus hash was computed over two files no one else had. Scan
# artifacts land in the cwd a scanner was run from, which is the root; specimen data never
# does. `!uv.lock` was an earlier patch to this same over-broad pattern.
/*.lock
!/uv.lock
